      {
        "pkcontributionId": 58219,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 25,
        "headingId": 229,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:17:06"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 397,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-11 coding",
    "headingPosition": 3,
    "headingid": 230,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 58220,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 25,
        "headingId": 230,
        "headingText": "<p>EE6Y Other specified fibromatous disorders of skin and soft tissue</p>",
        "timestamp": "2020-01-23T16:12:25"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 398,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Related terminology",
    "headingPosition": 4,
    "headingid": 231,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 58221,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 25,
        "headingId": 231,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Acceptable:</em> hyaline fibromatosis syndrome; infantile systemic hyalinosis.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:17:06"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 399,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Subtype(s)",
    "headingPosition": 5,
    "headingid": 232,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 58222,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 25,
        "headingId": 232,
        "headingText": "<p>None</p>",
        "timestamp": "2020-01-31T09:23:09"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 400,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 233,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 58223,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 25,
        "headingId": 233,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Hyaline fibromatosis syndrome is a multisystem disease (see below).</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:17:06"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 401,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 234,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 58224,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 25,
        "headingId": 234,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Hyaline fibromatosis syndrome typically presents in infancy but can occur later in childhood; more-severe cases present earlier {30176098; 22383261}. The most common clinical manifestations are subcutaneous&nbsp;/ soft tissue nodules and papules, gingival hypertrophy, and joint contractures (85&ndash;95% of cases), followed by other cutaneous and bone manifestations. Systemic/visceral involvement leading to failure to thrive, intractable diarrhoea (50% of cases), and recurrent infections (30% of cases) is defined as severe disease and is associated with early mortality {30176098; 22383261; 19344977}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:17:06"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 402,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 235,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 58225,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 25,
        "headingId": 235,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Hyaline fibromatosis syndrome demonstrates no sex predilection, following an autosomal recessive inheritance pattern.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:17:06"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 403,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 236,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 58226,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 25,
        "headingId": 236,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Juvenile hyaline fibromatosis is an autosomal recessive syndrome resulting from loss-of-function mutations, including inactivating homozygous or compound heterozygous mutations in the <em>ANTXR2</em> (<em>CMG2</em>) gene {29801470; 30176098}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:17:06"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 404,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 237,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 58227,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 25,
        "headingId": 237,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The <em>ANTXR2</em> gene encodes for a transmembrane protein that binds the extracellular proteins laminin and collagen&nbsp;VI; it also serves as a receptor for the anthrax toxin {22215446}. The nodules in hyaline fibromatosis syndrome patients are reported to contain collagen&nbsp;VI, suggesting that ANTXR2 may mediate its degradation. Therefore, in hyaline fibromatosis syndrome, given the loss of ANTXR2 function, collagen&nbsp;VI would accumulate in the extracellular matrix {28604699}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:17:06"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 411,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 244,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 58228,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 25,
        "headingId": 244,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The nodules are solid and homogeneous.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:17:06"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 405,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 238,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 58229,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 25,
        "headingId": 238,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Hyaline fibromatosis syndrome is characterized by hyaline-like extracellular matrix deposits. The lesions show variable cellularity and are composed of plump to spindled uniform fibroblasts, often arranged in cords simulating vascular spaces, admixed with abundant extracellular eosinophilic non-fibrillar hyaline material, obliterating normal structures {22383261; 15078358}. The hyaline material stains strongly with PAS and is diastase-resistant {22383261; 15078358}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:17:06"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 406,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 239,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 58230,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 25,
        "headingId": 239,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:17:06"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 407,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Diagnostic molecular pathology",
    "headingPosition": 14,
    "headingid": 240,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 58231,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 25,
        "headingId": 240,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Germline sequencing of <em>ANTXR2</em> (4q21.21) can confirm the diagnosis.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:17:06"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 410,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Essential and desirable diagnostic criteria",
    "headingPosition": 15,
    "headingid": 243,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 58232,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 25,
        "headingId": 243,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Essential:</em> subcutaneous nodules and gingival hypertrophy with abundant hyaline-like material.</p>\r\n<p><em>Desirable:</em> confirmation by germline sequencing of the <em>ANTXR2</em> gene.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:17:06"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 408,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Staging",
    "headingPosition": 16,
    "headingid": 241,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 58233,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 25,
        "headingId": 241,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:17:06"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 409,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Prognosis and prediction",
    "headingPosition": 17,
    "headingid": 242,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 58234,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 25,
        "headingId": 242,
        "headingText": "<p>Age at presentation is a predictor of prognosis, with earlier presentation associated with more-severe disease. In systemic/severe disease, the median age at death is 15&nbsp;months {30176098; 19344977; 22383261}. For patients with milder forms, wheelchair use may become necessary because of joint contractures. Phenotype&ndash;genotype correlations have demonstrated that <em>ANTXR2</em> missense mutations in exons&nbsp;1&ndash;12 and mutations leading to a premature stop codon more commonly lead to severe disease, whereas missense mutations in exons&nbsp;13&ndash;17 lead to a mild form of disease {30176098; 29801470; 22215446; 20331448}. Treatment is largely supportive. Surgical excision of subcutaneous nodules and/or gingivectomy for gingival hypertrophy is often performed; however, recurrences are common {30176098; 22383261}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T16:50:14"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  }
]